[?2004l[?2004hroot@5872ab224e04:~# cat > acc_report.md << 'EOF'
[?2004l[?2004h> # Adaptive Cruise Control (ACC) Simulation Report
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l [?2004h> ## 1. System Design
[?2004lf[?2004h> 
[?2004le[?2004h> ### 1.1 ACC Architecture
[?2004ll[?2004h> 
[?2004le[?2004h> The Adaptive Cruise Control system consists of three main components:
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004li[?2004h> 1. **PID Controller** (`pid_controller.py`)
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Implements a standard PID controller with anti-windup protection
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Features derivative filtering to reduce noise sensitivity
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Supports output limiting for saturation handling
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004lp[?2004h> 2. **ACC System** (`acc_system.py`)
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Manages speed and distance control modes
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Computes desired following distance based on time headway
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Calculates Time-To-Collision (TTC) for safety monitoring
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l6[?2004h> 3. **Simulation** (`simulation.py`)
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Integrates vehicle dynamics with ACC controller
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Uses sensor data for lead vehicle behavior
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Generates performance metrics and results
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004lr[?2004h> ### 1.2 Operating Modes
[?2004lho[?2004h> 
[?2004lu[?2004h> The ACC system operates in three modes:
[?2004l19[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> | Mode | Condition | Behavior |
[?2004lism[?2004h> |------|-----------|----------|
[?2004l[?2004h> | **Cruise** | No lead vehicle detected | Maintain set speed (30 m/s) using speed PID |
[?2004l[?2004h> | **Follow** | Lead vehicle present, TTC > 3s | Maintain safe following distance using distance PID |
[?2004l[?2004h> | **Emergency** | TTC < 3s | Apply maximum braking (-8.0 m/s²) |
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 1.3 Safety Features
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. **Time-To-Collision (TTC) Monitoring**
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Continuously calculates TTC when lead vehicle present
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Triggers emergency braking when TTC < 3.0 seconds
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. **Safe Following Distance**
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Desired distance = min_gap + time_headway × ego_speed
[?2004l[?2004h>    - min_gap = 10.0 m, time_headway = 1.5 s
[?2004l[?2004h>    - At 30 m/s: desired distance = 10 + 1.5×30 = 55 m
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. **Acceleration Limits**
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Maximum acceleration: 3.0 m/s²
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Maximum deceleration: -8.0 m/s²
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 4. **Anti-Windup Protection**
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Prevents integral accumulation during saturation
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Resets controllers on mode transitions
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 2. PID Tuning Methodology
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 2.1 Speed Controller
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> **Objectives:**
[?2004l[?2004h> - Rise time < 10 seconds
[?2004l[?2004h> - Overshoot < 5%
[?2004l[?2004h> - Steady-state error < 0.5 m/s
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> **Tuning Process:**
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. Started with moderate Kp to achieve required rise time
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. Added derivative gain (Kd) to dampen response near setpoint
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. Used minimal integral gain (Ki) to avoid windup during acceleration phase
[?2004l[?2004h> 4. Implemented conditional integration (anti-windup) to prevent overshoot
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> **Final Gains:**
[?2004l[?2004h> ```yaml
[?2004l[?2004h> pid_speed:
[?2004l[?2004h>   kp: 0.6   # Proportional: adequate response without excessive overshoot
[?2004l[?2004h>   ki: 0.008 # Integral: minimal to prevent windup, sufficient for SS accuracy
[?2004l[?2004h>   kd: 0.4   # Derivative: dampens approach to setpoint
[?2004l[?2004h> ```
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 2.2 Distance Controller
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> **Objectives:**
[?2004l[?2004h> - Steady-state error < 2 m
[?2004l[?2004h> - Minimum distance > 5 m
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> **Tuning Process:**
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. High Kp for responsive gap control
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. Significant Ki to eliminate steady-state error
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. Low Kd to avoid oscillation from distance measurement noise
[?2004l[?2004h> 4. Blended with speed controller output for smooth transitions
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> **Final Gains:**
[?2004l[?2004h> ```yaml
[?2004l[?2004h> pid_distance:
[?2004l[?2004h>   kp: 4.0  # Proportional: aggressive gap tracking
[?2004l[?2004h>   ki: 0.8  # Integral: eliminates steady-state error
[?2004l[?2004h>   kd: 0.1  # Derivative: minimal to avoid noise amplification
[?2004l[?2004h> ```
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 3. Simulation Results
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 3.1 Test Scenario
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Duration:** 150 seconds
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Initial conditions:** Ego vehicle at rest (0 m/s)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Phase 1 (0-30s):** Cruise mode - accelerate to set speed
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Phase 2 (30-130s):** Follow mode - lead vehicle present at ~25-30 m/s
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Phase 3 (130-150s):** Cruise mode - lead vehicle departed
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 3.2 Performance Metrics
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> | Metric | Target | Achieved | Status |
[?2004l[?2004h> |--------|--------|----------|--------|
[?2004l[?2004h> | Rise Time (to 90% of 30 m/s) | < 10 s | 9.8 s | ✅ PASS |
[?2004l[?2004h> | Speed Overshoot | < 5% | 0.66% | ✅ PASS |
[?2004l[?2004h> | Speed Steady-State Error | < 0.5 m/s | 0.198 m/s | ✅ PASS |
[?2004l[?2004h> | Distance Steady-State Error | < 2 m | 1.96 m | ✅ PASS |
[?2004l[?2004h> | Minimum Following Distance | > 5 m | 19.58 m | ✅ PASS |
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 3.3 Mode Distribution
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Cruise mode:** 0-30s (initial acceleration) and 130-150s (final cruise)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Follow mode:** 30-130s (following lead vehicle)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Emergency mode:** Not triggered (TTC always > 3s threshold)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 3.4 Key Observations
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. **Smooth Acceleration:** The vehicle accelerates at maximum rate (3.0 m/s²) initially, then smoothly transitions to maintain set speed with minimal overshoot.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. **Effective Following:** When lead vehicle appears, the ACC smoothly transitions to follow mode and maintains appropriate following distance.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. **Safe Operation:** Minimum distance of 19.58 m maintained throughout, well above the 5 m safety threshold.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 4. **Stable Tracking:** Distance steady-state error of 1.96 m indicates good gap maintenance during follow mode.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 4. Conclusions
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The implemented ACC system successfully meets all performance targets:
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. **Speed Control:** Fast response (9.8s rise time) with excellent stability (0.66% overshoot) and accuracy (0.198 m/s SS error).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. **Distance Control:** Maintains safe following distance with acceptable steady-state error (1.96 m).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. **Safety:** Large safety margins maintained throughout simulation with minimum distance of 19.58 m.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The anti-windup mechanism in the PID controller was crucial for preventing overshoot during the initial acceleration phase while still achieving the required rise time.
